  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will continue to build into the area today and be\ndirectly overhead tonight. Mostly sunny, breezy and dry weather\nis expected today, though winds will not be as strong as on\nMonday (gusts mainly 15 to 25 mph). Temperatures will run \n5 to 10 degrees below normal today with values ranging from the\nupper 40s across the Adirondacks and southern Greens to the\nlower 60s across the mid-Hudson Valley. A continued mostly clear\nsky combined with light to calm winds will allow for ideal \nradiational cooling conditions tonight with temperatures \nfalling rapidly at sunset. Some patchy high clouds may pass \nthrough across areas north of I-90 overnight but should do \nlittle to prevent cooling temperatures. Most valley areas will\nlikely fall into the low to mid-30s with some mid to upper 20s \nacross the Adirondacks and southern Vermont. This will allow for\nfrost to form across most of eastern New York and western New \nEngland, including the Hudson Valley, southern Taconics and \nLitchfield County, CT, where the growing season is ongoing. \nFreeze Warnings and Frost Advisories have been issued for these\nareas for tonight as confidence in these temperatures have \nincreased.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure shifts to the east by Wednesday allowing for\nsouth to southwesterly return flow to resume. This will allow\nfor more seasonable temperatures to return and continue through\nnext weekend. Longwave troughing sets up across the region \nlater this week as a series of weak disturbances track near the \nU.S./Canada border. This will allow for some light rain showers \nat times mainly for northern and western areas. Surface high \npressure and upper ridging builds in for next weekend with \ndry weather regionwide.",
